1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,575
The campaign that produced the champion.

2
00:00:02,575 --> 00:00:07,538
The method, the steps, the tools —
and two of the designs, in motion.

3
00:00:08,700 --> 00:00:13,138
The ancestry in one line:
Genie three made the backbones.

4
00:00:13,138 --> 00:00:17,125
Full-backbone ProteinMPNN
built families on the best one.

5
00:00:17,125 --> 00:00:19,750
A mutation round lifted the human record.

6
00:00:19,750 --> 00:00:22,750
A mouse-first redesign made it cross-reactive.

7
00:00:22,750 --> 00:00:26,000
Two selection generations
tightened both species.

8
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:28,650
And one histidine gave it the pH switch.

9
00:00:28,650 --> 00:00:31,400
Every link had a measured decision behind it.

10
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:34,238
First, calibration.

11
00:00:34,238 --> 00:00:41,038
We re-scored the released campaign — one hundred and fifty
designs — and reproduced their scores within three hundredths.

12
00:00:41,038 --> 00:00:47,650
The bar: below zero point eight, almost
no binders; at or above, a quarter bound.

13
00:00:47,650 --> 00:00:55,262
The generator decides more than the filter: Genie three made
eight of their twenty-three binders; ours, one of forty.

14
00:00:56,400 --> 00:01:06,662
The format came first: single small chains, the full trimer, the junction
of two protomers — the released hotspot pair, B one-oh-two and D sixty-two.

15
00:01:06,662 --> 00:01:12,200
Genie three then made the backbones — open
source, sixty-six seconds per design.

16
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:18,150
Batch one: eight designs, one clean
fold — d seven, sixty-four residues.

17
00:01:18,150 --> 00:01:23,588
Batch two: sixteen designs — the d
twelve family, ninety-two residues.

18
00:01:23,588 --> 00:01:26,362
Backbone choice dominates everything after it.

19
00:01:27,567 --> 00:01:28,854
Then the sequences.

20
00:01:28,854 --> 00:01:34,967
The first family came out capped — the CA-only lesson:
without the side chains, every score stays low.

21
00:01:34,967 --> 00:01:37,929
The fix: re-sequence the folded backbone.

22
00:01:37,929 --> 00:01:48,179
Eighteen variants of d seven — zero point six three to zero point eight five — every
fold at the designed site; a control backbone stayed below zero point one nine.

23
00:01:48,179 --> 00:01:51,279
Fold first; expand families on the winners.

24
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:54,450
Every candidate met the same gate.

25
00:01:54,450 --> 00:01:58,637
Boltz two co-folds it: five
samples, seed forty-two.

26
00:01:58,637 --> 00:02:06,775
I P S A E scores the interface at the designed two-protomer junction,
and a site check requires both halves of the epitope to touch.

27
00:02:06,775 --> 00:02:10,825
Score and site, together —
neither is evidence alone.

28
00:02:10,825 --> 00:02:12,375
Both designs passed.

29
00:02:13,533 --> 00:02:15,633
Two of the designs, in motion.

30
00:02:15,633 --> 00:02:22,746
D seven x t three dash three — real all-atom
molecular dynamics against human TNF-alpha.

31
00:02:22,746 --> 00:02:25,746
Pink is the binder; blue is the target.

32
00:02:25,746 --> 00:02:29,546
About three quarters of the interface
contacts hold through the run.

33
00:02:29,546 --> 00:02:32,308
A physics test of the pose, not an assay.

34
00:02:33,633 --> 00:02:41,046
The second design: b two x d twelve dash two —
the independent scaffold, ninety-two residues.

35
00:02:41,046 --> 00:02:43,158
Same treatment, same physics.

36
00:02:43,158 --> 00:02:47,996
Fifty-three interface pairs at the start,
and the contact count holds through the run.

37
00:02:47,996 --> 00:02:50,396
The backup sits where it was designed to sit.

38
00:02:51,700 --> 00:02:52,687
Then mouse.

39
00:02:52,687 --> 00:03:00,087
The first check said the family kept the designed interface on
mouse — but degraded, below the band of released mouse binders.

40
00:03:00,087 --> 00:03:07,388
So we stopped checking and started selecting: sixty-four
sequences designed against the mouse protein, sixteen folded.

41
00:03:07,388 --> 00:03:11,762
mce fourteen: the first design
strong on both species at once.

42
00:03:12,933 --> 00:03:15,296
Two selection generations locked it in.

43
00:03:15,296 --> 00:03:24,883
ds-mce-14-v6 — one substitution — at zero point eight
five six human and zero point eight five four mouse.

44
00:03:24,883 --> 00:03:29,096
The ds two family pushed the mouse
side to zero point eight six eight.

45
00:03:29,096 --> 00:03:34,596
Then the family plateaued — the pre-registered
move: change the input, not the volume.

46
00:03:35,700 --> 00:03:38,550
Now the switch — the objective ranked first.

47
00:03:38,550 --> 00:03:43,900
The linkage check found no switch at all —
slightly tighter in acid, the wrong direction.

48
00:03:43,900 --> 00:03:47,712
The retrofit: histidines
against the interface lysine.

49
00:03:47,712 --> 00:03:52,262
The first version, three histidines,
flipped the sign but cost binding.

50
00:03:52,262 --> 00:04:04,388
A position scan found the fix: at residue thirty-three, one histidine on the salt bridge kept the
binding — the strongest switch measured here, fifty-four point seven times toward pH seven point four.

51
00:04:05,567 --> 00:04:16,917
That is the champion: S R two, D S mce fourteen, V six, R two C
— one substitution, E thirty-three H, of the best dual design.

52
00:04:16,917 --> 00:04:23,017
Human zero point eight four nine, mouse zero
point eight four seven, five of five on both.

53
00:04:23,017 --> 00:04:28,779
Every switch ships with its paired control — the
lab subtracts everything except the histidine.

54
00:04:29,967 --> 00:04:37,142
Final tally: eight designs submitted — five switch
variants, two paired controls, and the human-target record.

55
00:04:37,142 --> 00:04:39,954
Everything is prediction
until the lab runs it.

56
00:04:39,954 --> 00:04:49,804
Tools: Genie three, ProteinMPNN, Boltz two, I P S A
E, PROPKA linkage, OpenMM dynamics, Mol star renders.
